          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graph+theory"} graph theory
            %strong orientation
            of an undirected
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graph"} graph
            is a choice of one of two possible directions for every
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/edge"} edge
            %strong oriented graph
            is an undirected graph equipped with an orientation.
          %p Since the precise definition of “undirected graph” can vary depending on context (notably, whether one allows loops or multiple edges), the precise definition of “oriented graph” is likewise context dependent.
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %h3#oriented_simple_graphs Oriented simple graphs
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/combinatorics"} combinatorics
          %p
            More formally: let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                      %mi V
                      %mn 2
                    %mo )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \binom{V}{2}
            denote the set of two-element subsets of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo ↪
                  %msup
                    %mi V
                    %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %mi V
                  %mo ×
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta \hookrightarrow V^2 = V \times V
            the diagonal
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi v
                    %mo ,
                    %mi v
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mi v
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{(v, v): v \in V\}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi q
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi V
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ∖
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                        %mi V
                        %mn 2
                      %mo )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q: V^2 \setminus \Delta \to \binom{V}{2}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                      %mi V
                      %mn 2
                    %mo )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \binom{V}{2}
            as the set of orbits of the fixed-point free involution
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↦
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x, y) \mapsto (y, x)
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi V
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ∖
                    %mi Δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V^2 \setminus \Delta
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo ,
                  %mi E
                  %mo ↪
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                      %mi V
                      %mn 2
                    %mo )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (V, E \hookrightarrow \binom{V}{2}
            together with a section of the quotient
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            identified in the pullback diagram
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi F
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mi V
                            %mn 2
                          %mo ∖
                          %mi Δ
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                            %mi p
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                            %mi q
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi E
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↪
                        %mtd
                          %mrow
                            %mo (
                            %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                              %mi V
                              %mn 2
                            %mo )
                          %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  F &amp; \to &amp; V^2 \setminus \Delta \\
                  \\mathllap{p} \downarrow &amp; &amp; \downarrow \mathrlap{q} \\
                  E &amp; \hookrightarrow &amp; \binom{V}{2}.
                  }
          %p
            Equivalently, an oriented graph consists of a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            and a subset
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mo ↪
                  %msup
                    %mi V
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E \hookrightarrow V^2
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x, y) \in E
            implies
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∉
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (y, x) \notin E
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x, y)
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            as an arrow from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/digraph"} digraph
            where 2-cycles are allowed (an arrow from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            and another back from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            to
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
          %h3#more_general_graph_orientations More general graph orientations
          %p
            If the underlying undirected graph is allowed to have loops and multiple edges, then an oriented graph is essentially the same thing as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+graph"} directed graph
            (i.e.,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quiver"} quiver
          %p
            Formally, let an undirected graph
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            be given by a quiver
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi s
                  %mo ,
                  %mi t
                  %mo :
                  %mi E
                  %mo ⇉
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s,t : E \rightrightarrows V
            together with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fixed+point"} fixed point
            free
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/involution"} involution
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo :
                  %mi E
                  %mo →
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i : E \to E
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi s
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi i
                  %mo =
                  %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s\circ i = t
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi i
                  %mo =
                  %mi s
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t \circ i = s
            (in the style of
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Serre1977"} Serre 1977
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            consists of a subset
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi E
                    %mo +
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E^+ \subseteq E
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjoint+union"} disjoint union
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi E
                      %mo +
                    %mo ⊎
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi E
                      %mo +
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E = E^+ \uplus i(E^+)
          %p
            Any such orientation induces a corresponding quiver
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi G
                      %mo +
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi E
                      %mo +
                    %mo ⇉
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G^+ = E^+ \rightrightarrows V
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mo ⇉
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E \rightrightarrows V
            can be seen as an orientation of the undirected Serre graph defined by adding a formal inverse to each edge.
          %p
            Although the terms “oriented graph” and “directed graph” become essentially equivalent under this reading, in many situations it is useful to consider an oriented graph as one of the many possible orientations of a given undirected graph. For example, various
            %span.newWikiWord
              graph invariants
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/graph+invariants"} ?
            (such as the
            = succeed "," do
              %span.newWikiWord
                flow polynomial
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/flow+polynomial"} ?
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/W.+T.+Tutte"} Tutte
            = succeed ")" do
              %span.newWikiWord
                Tutte polynomial
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Tutte+polynomial"} ?
